1. Mathematical Foundations

1.1 Elimination of Rotational Unbalance

The conventional washing machine derives its cleaning action from a rotating drum. The unavoidable consequence of this architecture is the centrifugal unbalance force generated by asymmetric mass distribution of the textile load:

F_{\text{unbal}} = m r \omega^2

For a representative domestic machine operating at 1,400 rpm (ω = 146.6 rad/s) with a 100 g residual imbalance at 0.1 m radius,  F_{\text{unbal}} \approx 215  N. This force is transmitted through the suspension system to the cabinet and floor. The transmissibility of the suspension is characterized by the standard isolation model:

T(f) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\left(1 - (f/f_n)^2\right)^2 + \left(2\zeta f/f_n\right)^2}}

where  f_n  is the natural frequency of the suspension and  \zeta  is the damping ratio. Experimental analysis of horizontal washing machines shows that isolation efficiency can reach approximately 96% with optimized suspension parameters, but this requires a compliant suspension that introduces geometric constraints and residual low-frequency motion. The stationary-chamber architecture removes the term  mr\omega^2  at its source. Any residual vibration arises only from actuator dynamics — microjet pumps operating at 10–20 Hz — which are two orders of magnitude lower in frequency and amplitude. The isolation problem, as classically formulated, ceases to exist.

1.2 Closed-Loop Capture with Fouling Dynamics

In a dilute-and-drain architecture, the contaminant concentration in the wash liquor rises monotonically. The mass balance is open-loop:

V \frac{dC_w}{dt} = R_{\text{detach}} - k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}} C_w

Redeposition is uncontrolled, and the process terminates at a fixed time irrespective of whether detachment has converged. The ATDS architecture operates in closed loop with continuous capture:

V \frac{dC_w}{dt} = R_{\text{detach}} - \eta_{\text{filter}} Q C_w - k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}} C_w

where  R_{\text{detach}}  is the detachment rate (kg/s),  Q  is the recirculation flow rate (m³/s), and  \eta_{\text{filter}}  is the single-pass capture efficiency. Closed-loop recirculation and filtration for laundry systems is established in prior art: US 9,879,368 and US 9,873,972 describe a system in which “the continuous, closed loop recirculation and filtration of the wash water provides a dynamic water exchange that maintains the wash water in the washing machine... in an improved state of cleanliness”. The fouling of the filter introduces a time-dependent efficiency:

\frac{dP_{\text{filter}}}{dt} \propto \frac{C_w Q}{A_{\text{filter}}}

where  P_{\text{filter}}  is the pressure drop across the filter. The design requirement is that  \eta_{\text{filter}}  remains above a specified threshold over the cycle duration, or that the filter is sized for the peak detachment rate  R_{\text{capture,min}} = \max_t R_{\text{detach}}(t)  rather than the average.

1.3 Chemo-Acoustic Kinetics

Ultrasound does not replace enzymatic action; it enhances it through two coupled mechanisms: mass-transfer intensification (sono-mechanics) and kinetic acceleration (sono-chemistry). The enzyme reaction rate in the presence of ultrasound is:

v = \frac{k_{\text{cat}} [E] [S]}{K_M + [S]} \cdot f(I, T)

where  f(I, T)  is an empirical enhancement function of acoustic intensity  I  (W/cm²) and temperature  T . The temperature dependence of the catalytic constant follows Arrhenius:

k_{\text{cat}} = A \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{R T}\right)

Experimental data from USDA research establishes that introducing a low-energy, uniform ultrasound field into enzyme processing solutions “greatly improved enzyme effectiveness, resulting in an increased reaction rate” without affecting fabric strength. The average improvement in enzyme performance caused by ultrasound ranges from 28% to 108% depending on fabric type. For wool scouring, the synergistic effect enables removal at 10 °C lower temperature and 15 minutes less time. The enhancement function can be modeled as:

f(I, T) = 1 + \frac{\alpha I}{K_I + I}

where  \alpha  is the maximum enhancement factor and  K_I  is the half-saturation intensity. Calibration requires measuring  k_{\text{cat}}  versus  I  at  I = 0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0  W/cm² and  T = 15, 20, 25  °C.

1.4 Cold-Adapted Enzyme Kinetics

The rate enhancement at low temperature is characterized by the ratio of catalytic constants:

R = \frac{k_{\text{cat}}(T_{\text{low}})}{k_{\text{cat}}(T_{\text{high}})} = \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{\text{low}}} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{high}}}\right)\right)

For a mesophilic enzyme with  E_a = 50  kJ/mol and a psychrophilic enzyme with  E_a = 30  kJ/mol, at  T_{\text{low}} = 288  K and  T_{\text{high}} = 313  K:

R_{\text{meso}} = e^{-50000/8.314 \cdot (1/288 - 1/313)} \approx 0.19

R_{\text{psychro}} = e^{-30000/8.314 \cdot (1/288 - 1/313)} \approx 0.37

The cold-adapted enzyme retains nearly twice the relative activity. Commercial cold-adapted proteases such as Kannase and Polarzyme are established in detergent formulations. However, cold-adapted enzymes often exhibit reduced thermal stability: the half-life  \tau_{1/2}  decreases at temperatures above 30 °C, which must be accounted for in the thermal management of the wash liquor. A trade-off function relates activity at low temperature to stability at elevated temperature:

\mathcal{T}(\text{enzyme}) = \left\{ \frac{k_{\text{cat}}(T_{\text{low}})}{k_{\text{cat}}^{\text{ref}}}, \frac{\tau_{1/2}(T_{\text{high}})}{\tau_{1/2}^{\text{ref}}} \right\}

The design objective is to select an enzyme that maximizes activity at the operating temperature while maintaining sufficient stability for the required cycle duration.

1.5 Microjet Shear and Darcy Penetration

A distributed array of micro-nozzles delivers high-shear jets to the textile surface. The wall shear stress from an impinging jet is:

\tau_{\text{jet}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2 \cdot f\left(\frac{r}{d}\right)

where  u_{\text{jet}} = \sqrt{2 \Delta P / \rho} . For  \Delta P = 0.1  MPa,  u_{\text{jet}} \approx 14  m/s and  \tau_{\text{jet}} \approx 10^5  Pa. The critical shear stress for detachment of particulate soils is  \tau_{\text{crit}} = F_{\text{adh}} / A_{\text{contact}} , which for typical soil-fiber adhesion forces ( F_{\text{adh}} \sim 10^{-9}  N) and contact areas ( A_{\text{contact}} \sim 10^{-12}  m²) yields  \tau_{\text{crit}} \sim 10^3  Pa. The jet exceeds this by two orders of magnitude.

However, textile is a porous medium. The penetration of the jet into the fabric is governed by Darcy’s law:

\mathbf{u} = -\frac{1}{\mu} \mathbf{K} \nabla P

where  \mathbf{K}  is the permeability tensor. For a fibrous porous medium, the through-plane permeability  k_{TP}  and in-plane permeability  k_{IP}  can be computed from fiber diameter  d_f  and solid volume fraction  \alpha_f  using the Spielman–Goren relations:

\frac{1}{4\alpha_f} = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{3k_{IP}}{2d_f} \frac{K_1(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{IP}})}{K_0(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{IP}})}

\frac{1}{4\alpha_f} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{2k_{TP}}{d_f} \frac{K_1(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{TP}})}{K_0(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{TP}})}

where  K_0  and  K_1  are modified Bessel functions of the second kind. For cotton with  k \sim 10^{-11}  m², a pressure differential of 0.1 MPa drives flow through a 1 mm fabric thickness in milliseconds, but the shear stress decays with depth. The detachment efficiency as a function of depth must be measured experimentally for representative soil–textile combinations.

1.6 Ozone Pre-Treatment Kinetics

Ozone decay in water follows first-order kinetics:

C_{\text{O3}}(t) = C_0 e^{-k_d t}

where  k_d  is the decay constant. The CT (concentration × time) value for microbial inactivation is:

\text{CT} = \int_0^t C_{\text{O3}}(t') \, dt'

The decay constant depends strongly on pH and temperature. Alkaline pH degrades ozone rapidly: “alkaline pH degraded ozone rapidly and yielded low inactivation of target bacteria, particularly when the temperature of the ozonated water was above refrigeration”. At neutral pH, water allows over 5 ppm ozone in the reactor, with residues detectable after 2 minutes of treatment; at high pH, ozone residuals are not detectable after one minute. The CT required for 3-log inactivation of E. coli at 20 °C is approximately 0.1–1 mg·min/L, but this value increases at lower temperatures and higher pH. Residual ozone must be destroyed before drain using activated carbon or UV treatment.

1.7 Plasma Surface Treatment: Dose and Byproducts

Plasma treatment is restricted to surface-limited odor and microbial reduction. The plasma dose is:

D_{\text{plasma}} = \int_0^t P_{\text{plasma}}(t') \, dt'

For 3-log microbial reduction, typical plasma doses are 0.1–1 kJ/cm². For a 1 m² textile surface,  D \approx 100–1000  J, comparable to the energy consumed by the jet pump. Plasma treatment of textiles generates ozone and nitrogen oxides (NO_x) as byproducts: “the formation of O3 and NOx in the downstream gases of air and oxygen plasmas may be responsible for the different effects on dye degradation”. The gas handling subsystem must include catalytic or adsorptive removal of these species before exhaust.

1.8 Probabilistic Decision Framework

The stop condition is probabilistic:

P\left(\eta_i \ge \eta_{i,\min} \ \forall i \mid y_{1:k}\right) \ge 1 - \delta

where  y_{1:k}  are sensor observations and  \delta  is the acceptable risk of premature termination. Damage and microplastic constraints are similarly probabilistic:

P(D > D_{\max}) \le \delta_D, \quad P(\text{MPF} > \text{MPF}_{\max}) \le \delta_M

The state estimator is a Kalman filter with state vector:

x = [C_{\text{text}}, C_w, T, \text{MPF}]^T

\dot{x} = f(x, u) + w, \quad y = H x + v

where  w \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q)  and  v \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R) . The prior from dry-load imaging initializes  x_0 ; effluent sensors update the estimate. The likelihood model for turbidity is  y_{\text{turb}} = a C_w + v ; for fluorescence,  y_{\text{fluo}} = b C_{\text{protein}} + v . Calibration of  a  and  b  is required under representative soil conditions.

1.9 Pareto Optimization

The objective vector is:

\mathbf{J} = [E_n, W_n, N_n, D_n, C_n, \text{MPF}_n]

The Pareto set is:

\mathcal{P} = \{x : \nexists y \text{ s.t. } J_i(y) \le J_i(x) \ \forall i, \text{ with strict inequality for at least one } i\}

Multi-objective optimization has been applied to washing machine suspension design — Pareto sets have been obtained for vibration minimization with different anti-vibration weight masses — but not to the cleaning process itself. The innovation is the application of Pareto analysis to the trade-offs between energy, water, noise, damage, cost, and microplastic generation.

1.10 Model Predictive Control with Probabilistic Constraints

At each time step, the controller solves:

\min_{u(\cdot)} \int_t^{t+T} \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{J}(x(\tau), u(\tau)) \, d\tau

subject to:

P(\eta_i \ge \eta_{i,\min}) \ge 1 - \delta

P(D > D_{\max}) \le \delta_D

P(\text{MPF} > \text{MPF}_{\max}) \le \delta_M

Stochastic MPC with probabilistic constraints is established in process control and has been applied to residential energy management with washing machine scheduling. The innovation is its application to the cleaning process itself, with a physics-based surrogate model for detachment, capture, and damage.

2. Architecture and Design

2.1 Stationary Chamber with Distributed Actuation

The rotating drum is replaced by a stationary textile chamber in which localized actuators apply mechanical, acoustic, and fluidic energy. The chamber geometry is designed to support the textile on a perforated platen, with actuator arrays positioned on the opposing face. The elimination of the rotating mass removes the dominant structural noise and vibration source. The EU ecodesign database shows regulatory pressure on noise, with the distribution of machines shifting toward quieter classes. The stationary architecture addresses this at the physical level: there is no unbalance force to attenuate.

2.2 Dual-Loop Jet and Capture Architecture

The single closed-loop of earlier conceptual designs is replaced by two independent loops:

Jet loop: high-pressure, low-volume.  Q_{\text{jet}} \sim 0.5  L/min,  \Delta P = 0.1  MPa. Delivers targeted shear to the textile surface through a micro-nozzle array.

Capture loop: low-pressure, high-volume.  Q_{\text{capture}} \sim 5  L/min,  \Delta P = 10  kPa. Draws contaminated liquor through staged filters (60 µm followed by 5 µm) and returns clarified liquor to the jet loop reservoir.

The separation prevents the filter from experiencing high-pressure pulses that cause plugging. Prior art on closed-loop laundry filtration explicitly notes that “the effective openings of the filters are chosen to reduce plugging which causes an undesirable increase in overall system pressure”. The dual-loop architecture allows independent optimization: the jet loop is sized for peak shear stress; the capture loop is sized for peak detachment rate.

2.3 Textile Fixation Geometry

The textile is tensioned on a perforated platen rather than tumbled. This provides defined spatial coordinates for the actuation field  u(x, y, z, t) , eliminates textile–textile abrasion that generates microplastic fibers, and enables optical sensing through a transparent platen. The trade-off is reduced load capacity: the fixation geometry constrains the volume of textile that can be processed per cycle. The microplastic generation term  K \tau^a  is reduced because the abrasive contact between opposing fabric surfaces is eliminated. Experimental validation requires measurement of MPF generation under tensioned fixation versus tumbling at equal cleaning performance, using the ISO 4484-3:2023 method for measuring collected material mass released from textile end products by domestic washing.

2.4 Chemo-Acoustic Reactor Design

The ultrasonic transducer is configured as a flow-through resonator. Textile passes 2–5 mm from the horn at 20 kHz, intensity 0.4–2 W/cm². Enzyme solution is injected upstream of the sonication zone. The residence time is controlled to match the optimum of the enhancement function  f(I, T) . This design directly implements the established result that ultrasound–enzyme systems achieve 90–92% wax removal and reduce processing time by approximately 30% compared with enzymatic treatment alone, while preserving fiber strength. The reactor is integrated into the jet loop, with the enzyme solution recirculated until the detachment rate converges.

2.5 Staged Sensing Architecture

Spatially resolved contamination mapping through opaque wet loads is not tractable. The sensing architecture is therefore staged:

Stage 1 — Dry-load prior: optical imaging or spectroscopic scanning of the dry load before wetting. This provides a prior estimate of contamination distribution and textile composition.

Stage 2 — In-process effluent monitoring: turbidity, UV-Vis absorbance, and fluorescence sensors in the capture loop measure contaminant concentration in real time.

Stage 3 — Model fusion: a Kalman filter fuses the prior with effluent observations to estimate the state vector  x = [C_{\text{text}}, C_w, T, \text{MPF}]^T . The stop condition is evaluated on the posterior distribution.

2.6 Capture Subsystem Design

The capture subsystem is sized for the peak detachment rate. The required capture rate is:

R_{\text{capture,min}} = \max_t R_{\text{detach}}(t)

The filter area is:

A_{\text{filter}} = \frac{R_{\text{capture,min}}}{\eta_{\text{filter}} Q}

For microplastic fiber capture, the emitted MPF is:

\text{MPF}_{\text{emitted}} = (1 - \eta_{\text{filter,MPF}}) \cdot \text{MPF}_{\text{generated}}

Published emission factors for domestic washing range from 124 to 308 mg per kg of fabric, corresponding to 640,000 to 1,500,000 fibers per kg. The most abundant fraction is retained by 60 µm filters. A capture efficiency of 99% would reduce emitted MPF to 1.24–3.08 mg/kg. The regulatory context is favorable: France has mandated microfiber filters on new washing machines by 2025, and the EU Strategy for Plastics is driving member-state action.

3. Technical Specifications

3.1 Energy Balance

The full energy balance for the ATDS cycle is:

E_{\text{total}} = E_{\text{pump,jet}} + E_{\text{pump,capture}} + E_{\text{us}} + E_{\text{ozone}} + E_{\text{control}} + E_{\text{sensor}}

For the jet pump, with  \Delta P = 0.1  MPa and  \eta_{\text{pump}} = 0.7 , the specific energy is  e_{\text{pump}} = \Delta P / \eta_{\text{pump}} \approx 143  kJ/m³. If the jet loop processes 5 L per kg textile,  E_{\text{pump,jet}} \approx 0.7  kJ/kg. The capture pump operates at lower pressure; for  \Delta P = 10  kPa and  Q = 5  L/min,  E_{\text{pump,capture}} \approx 0.1  kJ/kg. The ultrasonic transducer, operating at 2 W/cm² over 0.1 m² for 20 minutes, consumes  E_{\text{us}} \approx 24  kJ. The ozone generator consumes approximately 10–50 kJ per cycle. Control and sensing are negligible (< 1 kJ). The total auxiliary energy is conservatively estimated at 5–10 kJ/kg.

For comparison, heating 1 kg of water from 20 °C to 60 °C at 90% efficiency requires:

E_{\text{heat}} = \frac{m c_p \Delta T}{\eta} = \frac{1 \times 4.18 \times 40}{0.9} \approx 186 \text{ kJ/kg}

The ATDS cycle eliminates the thermal term entirely. Even with conservative auxiliary energy, the reduction exceeds 95%.

