Appendix B: Mathematical Derivations and Proofs

B.1 Unbalance Force and Transmissibility

B.1.1 Derivation of Centrifugal Unbalance Force

Consider a rotating drum of mass  M  with a point mass  m  displaced from the axis of rotation by distance  r . The drum rotates at angular velocity  \omega . The centrifugal force generated by the displaced mass is:

F_{\text{unbal}} = m r \omega^2

Proof. In the rotating reference frame, the mass  m  experiences a centrifugal acceleration  a_c = r\omega^2  directed radially outward. By Newton's second law,  F = ma , the force is  F_{\text{unbal}} = mr\omega^2 . In the inertial frame, this force manifests as a rotating radial force vector:

\mathbf{F}_{\text{unbal}}(t) = m r \omega^2 \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\omega t) \\ \sin(\omega t) \end{pmatrix}

The magnitude is constant; the direction rotates at frequency  \omega . \square

Numerical evaluation. For  m = 0.1  kg,  r = 0.1  m,  \omega = 2\pi \times 1400/60 = 146.6  rad/s:

F_{\text{unbal}} = 0.1 \times 0.1 \times (146.6)^2 = 214.9 \text{ N}

B.1.2 Transmissibility of Suspension

The equation of motion for a mass  M  supported by a spring  k  and damper  c , subject to base excitation  y(t) = Y e^{i\omega t} , is:

M\ddot{x} + c(\dot{x} - \dot{y}) + k(x - y) = 0

Let  z = x - y  be the relative displacement. Then:

M\ddot{z} + c\dot{z} + kz = -M\ddot{y} = M\omega^2 Y e^{i\omega t}

The steady-state solution is  z = Z e^{i\omega t}  with:

Z = \frac{M\omega^2 Y}{k - M\omega^2 + i c \omega}

The transmitted force to the base is  F_T = kz + c\dot{z} , so:

F_T = (k + i c\omega) Z = \frac{(k + i c\omega) M\omega^2 Y}{k - M\omega^2 + i c\omega}

The transmissibility is:

T(f) = \left| \frac{F_T}{F_0} \right| = \left| \frac{F_T}{M\omega^2 Y} \right| = \left| \frac{1 + i 2\zeta \frac{\omega}{\omega_n}}{1 - \left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)^2 + i 2\zeta \frac{\omega}{\omega_n}} \right|

where  \omega_n = \sqrt{k/M}  and  \zeta = c/(2\sqrt{kM}) . Taking the magnitude:

T(f) = \frac{\sqrt{1 + \left(2\zeta \frac{f}{f_n}\right)^2}}{\sqrt{\left(1 - \left(\frac{f}{f_n}\right)^2\right)^2 + \left(2\zeta \frac{f}{f_n}\right)^2}}

For  f/f_n \gg 1  (typical for washing machine suspensions,  f_n \approx 2–5  Hz,  f \approx 20–25  Hz), the transmissibility reduces to:

T(f) \approx \frac{2\zeta f/f_n}{(f/f_n)^2} = \frac{2\zeta}{f/f_n}

For  \zeta = 0.1 ,  f/f_n = 5 :

T \approx \frac{0.2}{5} = 0.04

This corresponds to 96% isolation efficiency. \square

Implication. The stationary chamber removes  F_{\text{unbal}}  at the source. The transmissibility function becomes irrelevant because there is no rotating unbalance to transmit.

