B.2 Closed-Loop Mass Balance: Analytical Solution and Convergence Proof

B.2.1 Governing Equation

The contaminant concentration in the wash liquor evolves according to:

V \frac{dC_w}{dt} = R_{\text{detach}} - \eta_{\text{filter}} Q C_w - k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}} C_w

Define the total removal coefficient:

\lambda = \eta_{\text{filter}} Q + k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}}

Then:

V \frac{dC_w}{dt} = R_{\text{detach}} - \lambda C_w

B.2.2 General Solution

For constant  R_{\text{detach}}  and  \lambda , the ODE is linear first-order:

\frac{dC_w}{dt} + \frac{\lambda}{V} C_w = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{V}

The integrating factor is  e^{\lambda t / V} :

\frac{d}{dt}\left(C_w e^{\lambda t / V}\right) = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{V} e^{\lambda t / V}

Integrating:

C_w(t) = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{\lambda} + \left(C_w(0) - \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{\lambda}\right) e^{-\lambda t / V}

The steady-state concentration is:

C_w(\infty) = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{\lambda} = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{\eta_{\text{filter}} Q + k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}}}

B.2.3 Convergence Proof

Theorem. For  \lambda > 0 ,  C_w(t) \to C_w(\infty)  exponentially as  t \to \infty , with time constant  \tau = V/\lambda .

Proof. From the general solution:

\left| C_w(t) - C_w(\infty) \right| = \left| C_w(0) - C_w(\infty) \right| e^{-\lambda t / V}

Since  \lambda > 0 , the exponential term decays monotonically to zero. For any  \epsilon > 0 , choose:

t > \frac{V}{\lambda} \ln\left(\frac{|C_w(0) - C_w(\infty)|}{\epsilon}\right)

Then  |C_w(t) - C_w(\infty)| < \epsilon . \square

B.2.4 Open-Loop vs. Closed-Loop Comparison

In the open-loop (conventional) case,  Q_{\text{fresh}} \gg Q_{\text{recirculated}} , so  \eta_{\text{filter}} \to 0 . The removal term vanishes:

V \frac{dC_w}{dt} = R_{\text{detach}} - k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}} C_w

The steady-state concentration becomes:

C_w^{\text{open}}(\infty) = \frac{R_{\text{detach}}}{k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}}}

Since  k_{\text{redep}} A_{\text{textile}} \ll \eta_{\text{filter}} Q  for an effective filter,  C_w^{\text{open}}(\infty) \gg C_w^{\text{closed}}(\infty) . The closed-loop system achieves lower equilibrium concentration and faster convergence. \square

B.2.5 Fouling Dynamics

The filter pressure drop increases as captured material accumulates:

\frac{dP_{\text{filter}}}{dt} = \frac{\alpha_f \mu C_w Q}{A_{\text{filter}}^2} P_{\text{filter}}

where  \alpha_f  is the specific cake resistance (m/kg) and  \mu  is the dynamic viscosity. This is the standard cake filtration equation. The solution is exponential:

P_{\text{filter}}(t) = P_0 \exp\left(\frac{\alpha_f \mu}{A_{\text{filter}}^2} \int_0^t C_w(t') Q \, dt'\right)

The design constraint is  P_{\text{filter}}(t_{\text{cycle}}) < P_{\max} , which determines the required filter area  A_{\text{filter}}  or the backwash interval.

