B.3 Chemo-Acoustic Kinetics: Derivation and Calibration

B.3.1 Enzyme Kinetics with Acoustic Enhancement

The Michaelis–Menten equation describes the enzyme reaction rate:

v = \frac{k_{\text{cat}} [E] [S]}{K_M + [S]}

In the presence of ultrasound, the effective rate becomes:

v_{\text{us}} = \frac{k_{\text{cat}} [E] [S]}{K_M + [S]} \cdot f(I, T)

where  f(I, T)  captures the acoustic enhancement. The enhancement function is modeled as:

f(I, T) = 1 + \frac{\alpha(T) I}{K_I + I}

where  \alpha(T)  is the maximum enhancement factor at temperature  T , and  K_I  is the half-saturation intensity.

B.3.2 Calibration Protocol

Objective. Determine  \alpha(T)  and  K_I  by measuring  k_{\text{cat}}  as a function of  I  and  T .

Procedure.

1. Prepare enzyme solution at fixed  [E]  and saturating  [S] \gg K_M . Then  v \approx k_{\text{cat}}[E] \cdot f(I,T) .
2. For each temperature  T \in \{15, 20, 25\}  °C, measure  v  at acoustic intensities  I \in \{0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0\}  W/cm².
3. Normalize:  f(I, T) = v(I, T) / v(0, T) .
4. Fit the model  f(I, T) = 1 + \alpha(T) I / (K_I + I)  using nonlinear least squares.
5. The temperature dependence of  \alpha  is modeled as Arrhenius:

\alpha(T) = \alpha_0 \exp\left(-\frac{E_{a,\alpha}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{ref}}}\right)\right)

B.3.3 Temperature Reduction Proof

Claim. If  f(I, T_{\text{low}}) > f(I, T_{\text{high}}) , then the same reaction rate can be achieved at lower temperature with ultrasound.

Proof. The reaction rate at temperature  T  without ultrasound is:

v_0(T) = A e^{-E_a/(RT)}

With ultrasound at intensity  I :

v_{\text{us}}(T) = A e^{-E_a/(RT)} \cdot f(I, T)

We seek  T_{\text{low}} < T_{\text{high}}  such that  v_{\text{us}}(T_{\text{low}}) = v_0(T_{\text{high}}) :

A e^{-E_a/(RT_{\text{low}})} \cdot f(I, T_{\text{low}}) = A e^{-E_a/(RT_{\text{high}})}

f(I, T_{\text{low}}) = e^{-\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{\text{high}}} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{low}}}\right)}

Since  T_{\text{low}} < T_{\text{high}} ,  1/T_{\text{high}} - 1/T_{\text{low}} < 0 , so the right side is less than 1. If  f(I, T_{\text{low}}) > 1 , a solution exists. \square

---

B.4 Cold-Adapted Enzyme Kinetics

B.4.1 Arrhenius Ratio Derivation

The ratio of catalytic constants at two temperatures is:

R = \frac{k_{\text{cat}}(T_{\text{low}})}{k_{\text{cat}}(T_{\text{high}})} = \frac{A e^{-E_a/(RT_{\text{low}})}}{A e^{-E_a/(RT_{\text{high}})}} = \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_{\text{low}}} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{high}}}\right)\right)

Proof. Direct substitution of the Arrhenius equation. \square

B.4.2 Numerical Comparison

For  T_{\text{low}} = 288  K,  T_{\text{high}} = 313  K:

\frac{1}{T_{\text{low}}} - \frac{1}{T_{\text{high}}} = \frac{1}{288} - \frac{1}{313} = 0.003472 - 0.003195 = 2.77 \times 10^{-4} \text{ K}^{-1}

For mesophilic enzyme ( E_a = 50,000  J/mol):

R_{\text{meso}} = \exp\left(-\frac{50000}{8.314} \times 2.77 \times 10^{-4}\right) = \exp(-1.666) = 0.189

For psychrophilic enzyme ( E_a = 30,000  J/mol):

R_{\text{psychro}} = \exp\left(-\frac{30000}{8.314} \times 2.77 \times 10^{-4}\right) = \exp(-0.999) = 0.368

The ratio of ratios:

\frac{R_{\text{psychro}}}{R_{\text{meso}}} = \frac{0.368}{0.189} = 1.95

The cold-adapted enzyme retains approximately twice the relative activity at low temperature. \square

B.4.3 Stability Trade-off

The half-life of enzyme activity at temperature  T  follows:

\tau_{1/2}(T) = \tau_0 \exp\left(\frac{E_{a,\text{den}}}{R T}\right)

where  E_{a,\text{den}}  is the denaturation activation energy. Cold-adapted enzymes typically have lower  E_{a,\text{den}} , meaning faster denaturation at elevated temperatures. The design constraint is:

\tau_{1/2}(T_{\text{operating}}) > t_{\text{cycle}}

This imposes an upper bound on operating temperature for a given enzyme.

---

B.5 Microjet Shear and Darcy Penetration

B.5.1 Impinging Jet Shear Stress

For a jet of velocity  u_{\text{jet}}  impinging on a flat plate, the stagnation pressure is:

P_{\text{stag}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2

The wall shear stress in the stagnation region scales as:

\tau_{\text{jet}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2 \cdot f\left(\frac{r}{d}\right)

where  f(r/d)  is a dimensionless function that depends on the radial position  r  normalized by nozzle diameter  d . For  r/d < 1 ,  f \approx 1 ; for  r/d > 1 ,  f  decays.

Proof. From Bernoulli's equation along a streamline from the nozzle exit to the stagnation point:

P_0 + \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2 = P_{\text{stag}} + 0

The dynamic pressure  \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2  is converted to static pressure at the stagnation point. The shear stress on the plate is proportional to the dynamic pressure through the boundary layer profile. \square

Numerical evaluation. For  \Delta P = 0.1  MPa,  \rho = 1000  kg/m³:

u_{\text{jet}} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \Delta P}{\rho}} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 10^5}{1000}} = 14.1 \text{ m/s}

\tau_{\text{jet}} = \frac{1}{2} \times 1000 \times (14.1)^2 = 9.94 \times 10^4 \text{ Pa} \approx 10^5 \text{ Pa}

B.5.2 Critical Detachment Shear Stress

The critical shear stress for detachment of a particle from a fiber is:

\tau_{\text{crit}} = \frac{F_{\text{adh}}}{A_{\text{contact}}}

For typical soil-fiber adhesion ( F_{\text{adh}} \sim 10^{-9}  N) and contact area ( A_{\text{contact}} \sim 10^{-12}  m²):

\tau_{\text{crit}} \sim 10^3 \text{ Pa}

The jet shear exceeds this by two orders of magnitude. \square

B.5.3 Darcy Penetration

For flow through a porous textile, Darcy's law gives:

\mathbf{u} = -\frac{1}{\mu} \mathbf{K} \nabla P

where  \mathbf{K}  is the permeability tensor. For a transversely isotropic fibrous medium, the through-plane permeability  k_{TP}  and in-plane permeability  k_{IP}  are related to fiber diameter  d_f  and solid volume fraction  \alpha_f  by the Spielman–Goren relations:

\frac{1}{4\alpha_f} = \frac{1}{4} + \frac{3k_{IP}}{2d_f} \frac{K_1(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{IP}})}{K_0(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{IP}})}

\frac{1}{4\alpha_f} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{2k_{TP}}{d_f} \frac{K_1(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{TP}})}{K_0(d_f / 2\sqrt{k_{TP}})}

where  K_0  and  K_1  are modified Bessel functions of the second kind.

Proof. These relations are derived by solving the Stokes equations for flow through a periodic array of cylinders and matching the pressure gradient to Darcy's law. The Bessel functions arise from the cylindrical geometry of the fibers. \square

Numerical evaluation. For cotton with  k_{TP} \sim 10^{-11}  m²,  \mu = 10^{-3}  Pa·s,  \Delta P = 10^5  Pa,  L = 10^{-3}  m:

u = \frac{k_{TP}}{\mu} \frac{\Delta P}{L} = \frac{10^{-11}}{10^{-3}} \frac{10^5}{10^{-3}} = 1 \text{ m/s}

The characteristic penetration time for depth  L  is:

t_{\text{pen}} \sim \frac{L}{u} = \frac{10^{-3}}{1} = 10^{-3} \text{ s} = 1 \text{ ms}

The jet penetrates the fabric in milliseconds, but the shear stress decays with depth. The detachment efficiency as a function of depth must be measured experimentally.

---

B.6 Ozone Pre-Treatment Kinetics

B.6.1 First-Order Decay

Ozone in water decomposes according to:

\frac{dC_{\text{O3}}}{dt} = -k_d C_{\text{O3}}

Solution:

C_{\text{O3}}(t) = C_0 e^{-k_d t}

Proof. Direct integration of the first-order ODE. \square

B.6.2 CT Value

The CT (concentration × time) value is:

\text{CT} = \int_0^t C_{\text{O3}}(t') \, dt' = \int_0^t C_0 e^{-k_d t'} \, dt' = \frac{C_0}{k_d} \left(1 - e^{-k_d t}\right)

For  t \gg 1/k_d :

\text{CT} \to \frac{C_0}{k_d}

B.6.3 Inactivation Requirement

For 3-log inactivation of E. coli at 20 °C,  \text{CT} \approx 0.1–1  mg·min/L. The required contact time is:

t = \frac{1}{k_d} \ln\left(\frac{C_0}{C_0 - k_d \cdot \text{CT}}\right)

For  C_0 = 3  ppm,  k_d = 0.1  min⁻¹,  \text{CT} = 0.5  mg·min/L:

t = \frac{1}{0.1} \ln\left(\frac{3}{3 - 0.1 \times 0.5}\right) = 10 \ln\left(\frac{3}{2.95}\right) = 10 \times 0.0168 = 0.168 \text{ min} \approx 10 \text{ s}

The contact time is short because ozone is highly reactive. However, the decay constant depends strongly on pH and organic load. At high pH,  k_d  increases and the required contact time decreases, but the residual ozone concentration drops faster, reducing efficacy.

B.6.4 Residual Ozone Destruction

The residual ozone must be destroyed before drain. The reaction with activated carbon is:

\text{O}_3 + \text{C} \to \text{CO}_2 + \text{O}_2

The design constraint is  C_{\text{O3,out}} < 0.1  ppm. The required carbon bed volume is:

V_{\text{carbon}} = \frac{Q \int_0^{t_{\text{cycle}}} C_{\text{O3}}(t') \, dt'}{q_{\text{carbon}}}

where  q_{\text{carbon}}  is the carbon adsorption capacity for ozone (typically 0.1–0.2 g O₃ per g carbon).

---

B.7 Plasma Dose and Byproduct Handling

B.7.1 Dose Calculation

The plasma dose is:

D_{\text{plasma}} = \int_0^t P_{\text{plasma}}(t') \, dt'

For a plasma power density  P_{\text{plasma}} = 1  W/cm² and treatment time  t = 100  s:

D_{\text{plasma}} = 1 \times 100 = 100 \text{ J/cm}^2

For a 1 m² textile surface:

D_{\text{total}} = 100 \times 10^4 = 10^6 \text{ J} = 1 \text{ MJ}

This is substantial: 1 MJ is comparable to the energy required to heat 2.4 kg of water from 20 °C to 60 °C. Plasma treatment is therefore energy-intensive and should be restricted to surface-limited applications where its unique chemistry (reactive oxygen and nitrogen species) provides a functional benefit that cannot be achieved by other means.

B.7.2 Byproduct Formation

Plasma treatment of textiles in air generates ozone and nitrogen oxides. The reactions are:

\text{O}_2 + e^- \to 2\text{O} + e^-

\text{O} + \text{O}_2 + M \to \text{O}_3 + M

\text{N}_2 + e^- \to 2\text{N} + e^-

\text{N} + \text{O}_2 \to \text{NO} + \text{O}

\text{NO} + \text{O}_3 \to \text{NO}_2 + \text{O}_2

The exhaust gas must be treated with a catalytic converter or adsorber. The design constraint is  \text{NO}_x < 0.1  ppm and  \text{O}_3 < 0.1  ppm at the exhaust.

