B.8 Kalman Filter Derivation

B.8.1 State-Space Model

The continuous-time state-space model is:

\dot{x} = f(x, u) + w, \quad w \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q)

y = H x + v, \quad v \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R)

where  x = [C_{\text{text}}, C_w, T, \text{MPF}]^T .

B.8.2 Discretization

For a sampling interval  \Delta t , the discrete-time model is:

x_{k+1} = F_k x_k + B_k u_k + w_k

y_k = H_k x_k + v_k

where  F_k = I + A \Delta t ,  A = \partial f / \partial x ,  B_k = B \Delta t .

B.8.3 Prediction Step

The predicted state estimate is:

\hat{x}_{k|k-1} = F_{k-1} \hat{x}_{k-1|k-1} + B_{k-1} u_{k-1}

The predicted covariance is:

P_{k|k-1} = F_{k-1} P_{k-1|k-1} F_{k-1}^T + Q_{k-1}

B.8.4 Update Step

The Kalman gain is:

K_k = P_{k|k-1} H_k^T \left(H_k P_{k|k-1} H_k^T + R_k\right)^{-1}

The updated state estimate is:

\hat{x}_{k|k} = \hat{x}_{k|k-1} + K_k \left(y_k - H_k \hat{x}_{k|k-1}\right)

The updated covariance is:

P_{k|k} = (I - K_k H_k) P_{k|k-1}

Proof. The Kalman filter is the minimum-variance unbiased estimator for linear Gaussian systems. The derivation follows from minimizing the trace of the posterior covariance  P_{k|k}  subject to the constraint that the estimator is unbiased. \square

B.8.5 Likelihood Model

For turbidity:

y_{\text{turb}} = a C_w + v_{\text{turb}}, \quad v_{\text{turb}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{\text{turb}}^2)

For fluorescence:

y_{\text{fluo}} = b C_{\text{protein}} + v_{\text{fluo}}, \quad v_{\text{fluo}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{\text{fluo}}^2)

The calibration constants  a  and  b  are determined experimentally by preparing wash liquor samples with known contaminant concentrations and measuring the sensor response.

---

B.9 Pareto Optimality

B.9.1 Definition

A point  x^* \in \mathcal{X}  is Pareto optimal if there is no  x \in \mathcal{X}  such that:

J_i(x) \le J_i(x^*) \quad \forall i \in \{1, \ldots, m\}

with strict inequality for at least one  i .

B.9.2 Existence Proof

Theorem. If  \mathcal{X}  is compact and  J_i  are continuous, then the Pareto set  \mathcal{P}  is non-empty.

Proof. Since  \mathcal{X}  is compact and  J_i  are continuous, the image  J(\mathcal{X})  is compact in  \mathbb{R}^m . A compact subset of  \mathbb{R}^m  has a minimal element under the partial order defined by componentwise inequality. This minimal element corresponds to a Pareto-optimal point. \square

B.9.3 Weighted Scalarization

A Pareto-optimal point can be found by solving:

\min_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{i=1}^m w_i J_i(x)

for some weight vector  w \in \mathbb{R}^m_+ . The solution is Pareto optimal if  w_i > 0  for all  i .

Proof. Suppose  x^*  minimizes the weighted sum and is not Pareto optimal. Then there exists  x  with  J_i(x) \le J_i(x^*)  for all  i  and strict inequality for some  j . Multiplying by  w_i > 0  and summing:

\sum_i w_i J_i(x) < \sum_i w_i J_i(x^*)

This contradicts the assumption that  x^*  minimizes the weighted sum. \square

---

B.10 Model Predictive Control Stability

B.10.1 Lyapunov Stability

Consider the MPC problem:

\min_{u(\cdot)} \int_t^{t+T} \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{J}(x(\tau), u(\tau)) \, d\tau

subject to the system dynamics and probabilistic constraints.

Theorem. If the terminal cost  V_f(x(t+T))  is a control Lyapunov function and the terminal set  \mathcal{X}_f  is invariant, then the closed-loop system is asymptotically stable.

Proof. Let  V(x)  be the optimal value function. By the principle of optimality:

V(x(t)) = \min_u \left\{ \int_t^{t+\Delta t} \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{J} \, d\tau + V(x(t+\Delta t)) \right\}

If the terminal cost is a control Lyapunov function, then  V(x(t+\Delta t)) < V(x(t))  for  x \neq 0 . Therefore  V  decreases along closed-loop trajectories, establishing asymptotic stability. \square

