B.11 Full Energy Balance

B.11.1 Component Energies

The total energy is:

E_{\text{total}} = E_{\text{pump,jet}} + E_{\text{pump,capture}} + E_{\text{us}} + E_{\text{ozone}} + E_{\text{control}} + E_{\text{sensor}}

Jet pump:

E_{\text{pump,jet}} = \frac{\Delta P_{\text{jet}} V_{\text{jet}}}{\eta_{\text{pump}}}

For  \Delta P_{\text{jet}} = 0.1  MPa,  V_{\text{jet}} = 5  L/kg,  \eta_{\text{pump}} = 0.7 :

E_{\text{pump,jet}} = \frac{10^5 \times 5 \times 10^{-3}}{0.7} = 714 \text{ J/kg} = 0.714 \text{ kJ/kg}

Capture pump:

E_{\text{pump,capture}} = \frac{\Delta P_{\text{capture}} V_{\text{capture}}}{\eta_{\text{pump}}}

For  \Delta P_{\text{capture}} = 10  kPa,  V_{\text{capture}} = 20  L/kg,  \eta_{\text{pump}} = 0.7 :

E_{\text{pump,capture}} = \frac{10^4 \times 20 \times 10^{-3}}{0.7} = 286 \text{ J/kg} = 0.286 \text{ kJ/kg}

Ultrasonic transducer:

E_{\text{us}} = I A_{\text{us}} t_{\text{us}}

For  I = 1  W/cm²,  A_{\text{us}} = 1000  cm²,  t_{\text{us}} = 1200  s:

E_{\text{us}} = 1 \times 1000 \times 1200 = 1.2 \times 10^6 \text{ J} = 1.2 \text{ MJ}

For a 5 kg load:  E_{\text{us}} = 240  kJ/kg. This is the dominant auxiliary energy term.

Ozone generator:

E_{\text{ozone}} = P_{\text{ozone}} t_{\text{ozone}}

For  P_{\text{ozone}} = 50  W,  t_{\text{ozone}} = 600  s:

E_{\text{ozone}} = 50 \times 600 = 30,000 \text{ J} = 30 \text{ kJ}

For a 5 kg load:  E_{\text{ozone}} = 6  kJ/kg.

Control and sensing:

E_{\text{control}} + E_{\text{sensor}} \approx 1 \text{ kJ/kg}

B.11.2 Total Auxiliary Energy

E_{\text{total}} = 0.714 + 0.286 + 240 + 6 + 1 = 248 \text{ kJ/kg}

This is dominated by the ultrasonic transducer. If the ultrasonic power is reduced to 0.4 W/cm² (the lower end of the effective range),  E_{\text{us}} = 96  kJ/kg, and the total becomes:

E_{\text{total}} = 0.714 + 0.286 + 96 + 6 + 1 = 104 \text{ kJ/kg}

B.11.3 Comparison with Conventional

For a conventional machine heating 15 L of water from 20 °C to 60 °C:

E_{\text{heat}} = \frac{m c_p \Delta T}{\eta} = \frac{15 \times 4.18 \times 40}{0.9} = 2787 \text{ kJ}

For a 5 kg load:  E_{\text{heat}} = 557  kJ/kg.

The ATDS auxiliary energy (104–248 kJ/kg) is 19–45% of the conventional thermal energy. The reduction is 55–81%, not the >95% claimed if only the jet pump is counted. The ultrasonic energy is the critical term. Optimization of the acoustic dose is essential.

---

B.12 Microplastic Generation Model

B.12.1 Empirical Power Law

The microplastic fiber generation rate is modeled as:

\text{MPF} = K \cdot \tau^a \cdot T^b \cdot t^c

where  \tau  is the mechanical shear stress,  T  is temperature,  t  is time, and  K, a, b, c  are textile-specific constants.

Evidence. Published emission factors for domestic washing range from 124 to 308 mg per kg of fabric, corresponding to 640,000 to 1,500,000 fibers per kg. The most abundant fraction is retained by 60 µm filters.

B.12.2 Fixation vs. Tumbling

In a tumbling machine, the textile experiences repeated impact and abrasion. The effective shear stress is:

\tau_{\text{tumble}} = \rho_{\text{fabric}} g h_{\text{fall}} \cdot f_{\text{impact}}

where  h_{\text{fall}}  is the fall height and  f_{\text{impact}}  is the impact frequency. In a tensioned fixation geometry, the textile is stationary and the shear stress is applied by the jet:

\tau_{\text{fix}} = \tau_{\text{jet}} = \frac{1}{2} \rho u_{\text{jet}}^2

The ratio of MPF generation is:

\frac{\text{MPF}_{\text{fix}}}{\text{MPF}_{\text{tumble}}} = \left(\frac{\tau_{\text{jet}}}{\tau_{\text{tumble}}}\right)^a

If  \tau_{\text{jet}} > \tau_{\text{tumble}}  but the exposure time is shorter and the abrasive textile–textile contact is eliminated, the net MPF generation may be lower. Experimental validation is required.

---

B.13 Filter Sizing

B.13.1 Peak Capture Rate

The required capture rate is:

R_{\text{capture,min}} = \max_t R_{\text{detach}}(t)

The detachment rate is highest at the beginning of the cycle when the contaminant concentration on the textile is highest. The peak detachment rate can be estimated from the initial contamination load  M_0  and the characteristic detachment time  \tau_{\text{detach}} :

R_{\text{detach,peak}} \sim \frac{M_0}{\tau_{\text{detach}}}

For  M_0 = 10  g and  \tau_{\text{detach}} = 60  s:

R_{\text{detach,peak}} \sim \frac{0.01}{60} = 1.67 \times 10^{-4} \text{ kg/s}

B.13.2 Filter Area

The filter area is:

A_{\text{filter}} = \frac{R_{\text{capture,min}}}{\eta_{\text{filter}} Q C_w}

For  R_{\text{capture,min}} = 1.67 \times 10^{-4}  kg/s,  \eta_{\text{filter}} = 0.95 ,  Q = 5  L/min =  8.33 \times 10^{-5}  m³/s,  C_w = 1  kg/m³:

A_{\text{filter}} = \frac{1.67 \times 10^{-4}}{0.95 \times 8.33 \times 10^{-5} \times 1} = 2.11 \text{ m}^2

This is a large filter area for a domestic appliance. The design must balance capture efficiency, flow rate, and pressure drop. A staged filter (60 µm followed by 5 µm) allows the primary filter to capture the bulk of the material at lower pressure drop, while the secondary filter polishes the liquor.

---

B.14 Closed-Loop Convergence Proof

Theorem. Under the closed-loop control law:

u(t) = \arg\min_u \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{J}(x, u)

subject to  P(\eta_i \ge \eta_{i,\min}) \ge 1 - \delta , the state  x(t)  converges to the feasible set  \mathcal{F} = \{x : \eta_i(x) \ge \eta_{i,\min} \ \forall i\}  in finite time with probability  1 - \delta .

Proof. The controller minimizes the objective  \mathbf{J}  subject to the probabilistic constraint. The constraint defines a feasible set  \mathcal{F} . The objective  \mathbf{J}  is lower-bounded (energy, water, and time are non-negative). The controller reduces  \mathbf{J}  at each step until either the constraint is satisfied or  \mathbf{J}  reaches its lower bound. Since the constraint is feasible by assumption, the controller reaches  \mathcal{F}  in finite time. The probabilistic constraint is satisfied with probability  1 - \delta  by construction. \square

