Appendix D: Calibration Protocols

D.1 Enzyme Kinetics Calibration

Objective: Determine  \alpha(T)  and  K_I  for the chemo-acoustic enhancement function.

Materials:

· Purified enzyme (e.g., subtilisin, lipase)
· Chromogenic substrate (e.g., succinyl-Ala-Ala-Pro-Phe-p-nitroanilide for protease)
· Buffer (pH 8.0, 50 mM Tris-HCl)
· Temperature-controlled ultrasonic bath (20 kHz, variable power)

Procedure:

1. Prepare enzyme solution at 10 nM in buffer.
2. Prepare substrate solution at 1 mM in buffer.
3. For each temperature  T \in \{15, 20, 25, 30, 35\}  °C:
   · Equilibrate both solutions for 10 min.
   · Mix 100 µL enzyme with 900 µL substrate.
   · Immediately place in ultrasonic bath at intensity  I \in \{0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0\}  W/cm².
   · Measure absorbance at 410 nm every 10 s for 5 min.
4. Calculate initial rate  v_0  from the linear portion of the absorbance vs. time curve.
5. Normalize:  f(I, T) = v_0(I, T) / v_0(0, T) .
6. Fit  f(I, T) = 1 + \alpha(T) I / (K_I + I)  using nonlinear least squares.
7. Fit  \alpha(T) = \alpha_0 \exp(-E_{a,\alpha}/R \cdot (1/T - 1/T_{\text{ref}})) .

D.2 Sensor Calibration

Objective: Determine  a  and  b  for turbidity and fluorescence likelihood models.

Procedure:

1. Prepare wash liquor samples with known concentrations of:
   · Particulate soil (e.g., kaolin, carbon black)
   · Protein soil (e.g., bovine serum albumin)
   · Oil soil (e.g., olive oil, sebum)
2. Measure turbidity (NTU) and fluorescence (excitation 280 nm, emission 340 nm).
3. Fit linear models:
   ·  y_{\text{turb}} = a C_w + v_{\text{turb}} 
   ·  y_{\text{fluo}} = b C_{\text{protein}} + v_{\text{fluo}} 
4. Determine noise variances  \sigma_{\text{turb}}^2  and  \sigma_{\text{fluo}}^2  from replicate measurements.
5. Validate on independent samples.

D.3 Filter Efficiency Calibration

Objective: Determine  \eta_{\text{filter}}  as a function of particle size and loading.

Procedure:

1. Prepare suspension of microplastic fibers (e.g., polyester, nylon) with known size distribution.
2. Circulate through filter at controlled flow rate  Q .
3. Measure influent and effluent concentrations using:
   · Optical particle counter
   · Filtration and gravimetric analysis
   · Microspectroscopy (FTIR, Raman)
4. Calculate  \eta_{\text{filter}} = 1 - C_{\text{out}} / C_{\text{in}} .
5. Repeat at different loading levels to characterize fouling.
6. Fit  \eta_{\text{filter}}(d_p, t)  model.

D.4 MPF Generation Calibration

Objective: Determine  K, a, b, c  for the microplastic generation model.

Procedure:

1. Select representative textiles (cotton, polyester, blends).
2. Wash under controlled conditions:
   · Mechanical action: tumbling vs. tensioned fixation
   · Temperature:  T \in \{20, 30, 40, 60\}  °C
   · Time:  t \in \{30, 60, 90, 120\}  min
3. Collect all effluent and filter through 60 µm, 5 µm, and 0.45 µm filters.
4. Measure MPF mass and count using ISO 4484-3:2023.
5. Fit power-law model  \text{MPF} = K \tau^a T^b t^c .
6. Validate on independent textile samples.

