Appendix I: Proof of Architectural Optimality

I.1 Statement

Theorem. The ATDS architecture is Pareto-optimal with respect to the objective vector  \mathbf{J} = [E_n, W_n, N_n, D_n, C_n, \text{MPF}_n]  under the constraint of equivalent cleaning performance.

I.2 Proof Sketch

Assumptions:

1. Cleaning performance  \eta  is fixed at a required level  \eta_{\min} .
2. The conventional architecture is characterized by:
   · Rotating drum:  E_{\text{heat}} = m c_p \Delta T / \eta ,  N_{\text{unbal}} = m r \omega^2 ,  \text{MPF} = K \tau^a T^b t^c .
   · Open-loop water:  W_{\text{conv}} = V_{\text{fill}} + V_{\text{rinse}} .
3. The ATDS architecture is characterized by:
   · Stationary chamber:  N_{\text{unbal}} = 0 ,  E_{\text{heat}} = 0 ,  \text{MPF} = K \tau_{\text{jet}}^a T_{\text{low}}^b t^c .
   · Closed-loop capture:  W_{\text{ATDS}} = V_{\text{initial}} + V_{\text{makeup}} .

Proof.

1. Noise:  N_{\text{ATDS}} = 0 < N_{\text{conv}} . ATDS is strictly better.
2. Energy:  E_{\text{ATDS}} = E_{\text{aux}}  vs.  E_{\text{conv}} = E_{\text{heat}} + E_{\text{mech}} . Since  E_{\text{heat}} \gg E_{\text{aux}}  for  \Delta T > 20  °C,  E_{\text{ATDS}} < E_{\text{conv}} .
3. Water:  W_{\text{ATDS}} = V_{\text{initial}} + V_{\text{makeup}}  vs.  W_{\text{conv}} = V_{\text{fill}} + V_{\text{rinse}} . Since  V_{\text{makeup}} \ll V_{\text{rinse}} ,  W_{\text{ATDS}} < W_{\text{conv}} .
4. Damage:  D_{\text{ATDS}}  is lower due to elimination of textile–textile abrasion and lower temperature.
5. Cost:  C_{\text{ATDS}}  depends on component costs; may be higher initially but lower operational cost.
6. MPF:  \text{MPF}_{\text{ATDS}}  is lower due to fixation geometry and capture.

Since ATDS is strictly better on at least two objectives (noise and MPF) and no worse on others (under the stated assumptions), it is Pareto-optimal. \square

I.3 Caveats

The proof relies on assumptions that require experimental validation:

·  E_{\text{aux}} \ll E_{\text{heat}}  — validated by energy balance (Appendix B.11).
·  V_{\text{makeup}} \ll V_{\text{rinse}}  — requires filter performance validation.
·  D_{\text{ATDS}} < D_{\text{conv}}  — requires damage testing.
·  \text{MPF}_{\text{ATDS}} < \text{MPF}_{\text{conv}}  — requires ISO 4484-3 validation.

If any of these assumptions fail, the Pareto dominance claim must be revised.

