Appendix S: Proof of Pareto Dominance Under Uncertainty

S.1 Statement

Theorem. If the ATDS objective vector  \mathbf{J}_{\text{ATDS}}  is Pareto-dominant over  \mathbf{J}_{\text{conv}}  in expectation, and the uncertainty in  \mathbf{J}  is bounded, then ATDS is Pareto-dominant with probability  1 - \delta  for some  \delta .

S.2 Proof

Let  \mathbf{J}_{\text{ATDS}} = \boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{ATDS}} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\text{ATDS}}  and  \mathbf{J}_{\text{conv}} = \boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{conv}} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\text{conv}} , where  \boldsymbol{\mu}  are the expected values and  \boldsymbol{\epsilon}  are zero-mean random errors with bounded support.

If  \boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{ATDS}} \le \boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{conv}}  componentwise with strict inequality for at least one component, then by continuity of the probability measure, there exists  \delta > 0  such that:

P\left(\mathbf{J}_{\text{ATDS}} \le \mathbf{J}_{\text{conv}}\right) \ge 1 - \delta

The value of  \delta  depends on the magnitude of the errors and the gap between the means. If the gap is large compared to the error standard deviations,  \delta  is small. \square

S.3 Implication

The ATDS architecture is robustly Pareto-dominant if the expected performance improvements are large compared to the uncertainties. The probabilistic framework ensures that the risk of non-dominance is quantified and controlled.

