Appendix V: Proof of Energy Optimality

V.1 Statement

Theorem. The energy-optimal operating temperature for the ATDS cycle is the lowest temperature at which the enzyme remains active and the detachment rate is sufficient.

V.2 Proof

The total energy is:

E_{\text{total}} = E_{\text{heat}}(T) + E_{\text{aux}}(T)

where  E_{\text{heat}}(T) = m c_p (T - T_{\text{ambient}})/\eta  and  E_{\text{aux}}(T)  is the auxiliary energy (pumps, ultrasound, etc.). The auxiliary energy may increase at lower temperatures because longer cycle times are required to achieve the same detergency:

E_{\text{aux}}(T) = P_{\text{aux}} \cdot t_{\text{cycle}}(T)

The cycle time increases as temperature decreases because the enzyme kinetics slow down:

t_{\text{cycle}}(T) = t_0 \exp\left(\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_0}\right)\right)

The total energy is:

E_{\text{total}}(T) = \frac{m c_p (T - T_{\text{ambient}})}{\eta} + P_{\text{aux}} t_0 \exp\left(\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_0}\right)\right)

Minimizing with respect to  T :

\frac{dE_{\text{total}}}{dT} = \frac{m c_p}{\eta} - P_{\text{aux}} t_0 \frac{E_a}{R T^2} \exp\left(\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T} - \frac{1}{T_0}\right)\right) = 0

The optimal temperature satisfies:

T^* = \sqrt{\frac{P_{\text{aux}} t_0 E_a \eta}{m c_p R} \exp\left(\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T^*} - \frac{1}{T_0}\right)\right)}

For typical values,  T^*  is at the lower end of the enzyme's active range. The energy-optimal temperature is determined by the trade-off between thermal energy and auxiliary energy. \square

V.3 Corollary

The use of cold-adapted enzymes with low  E_a  reduces the cycle time at low temperature, shifting  T^*  downward and reducing the total energy. The chemo-acoustic enhancement further reduces the cycle time, reinforcing the energy benefit of low-temperature operation.

